    "q-160638": {
      "dom_id": "step-q160638",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{{e}^{x}},\\,y=\\frac{12}{{e}^{x}},\\,2y-1=0,\\,2y-5=0\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}y=5,\\,{e}^{x}y=12,\\,2y=1,\\,2y=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x}y,\\,v=2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={e}^{x}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}y=5\\,\\text{ }\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x}y=12\\,⟹\\,u=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2y=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 12,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14186104/assets/615d6822e60e9b5ff646497e14df1468e1fd09ee67fab5c334b635d130f16f14.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}y & {e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}y \\\\  & =2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{12}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
